// src/user/compositor/ime_router.pdx -- G11-M3-001 (paideia-os #2316)
//
// KIND_IME_ROUTER = 0x1DE, non-linear derived cap over
// KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT (= 5).  Design authority: design/compositor/
// pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.20 KIND_IME_ROUTER.  Sibling landings in
// this same G11 wave:
//   * §2.18 KIND_IME_SESSION  -- src/user/ime/session_kind.pdx
//                                (G11-M1-001, #2314 -- LANDED B8, LINEAR)
//   * §2.19 KIND_IME_PROVIDER -- src/user/ime/provider_kind.pdx
//                                (G11-M2-001, #2315 -- LANDED B10, non-linear)
//   * §2.20 KIND_IME_ROUTER   -- THIS FILE (G11-M3-001, B11)
//
// This landing closes the trio: the ROUTER is the SINGLE, EXCLUSIVE
// authority that owns the keystroke-to-IME contract inside the
// compositor.  It holds the (seat, active_session, active_provider)
// triple and is the ONLY path a keystroke can take on its way to
// preedit / commit.  Novel-clean: NO BYPASS PATH.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  DESIGN COMMITMENT  --  ONE ROUTER, ONE CONTRACT, NO BYPASS
// =============================================================================
//
// IBus / Fcitx / SCIM on Linux allow "shortcut keys" (a per-app allowlist
// of key combos, e.g. Ctrl+Space, Super, Left/Right arrow inside a
// candidate window, media keys, ...) to be dispatched STRAIGHT to the
// application without passing through the IME session.  The rationale
// is user-facing: nobody wants Ctrl+A to become an IME sub-mode toggle
// in a text editor.  The consequence is architectural: every keystroke
// now takes ONE OF TWO paths (IME vs bypass), and every layer that
// touches the keyboard event stream has to know the current bypass
// state.  Pitfall P10 in `design/compositor/next-wave-synthesis.md`
// names this exact bug class -- two authorities racing over the same
// keystroke; a session that thought it owned the composition getting
// its state clobbered by a "bypassed" key that the toolkit interpreted
// unilaterally.
//
// This landing pins the counter-design: EVERY keystroke goes through
// the router.  Full stop.  Applications that want "Ctrl+A = select all"
// negotiate that at the toolkit layer AFTER the router has (a) delivered
// the keystroke to the active session and (b) received the session's
// response indicating the key was not consumed as part of a composition.
// The IME session ALWAYS gets first look; the compositor's own
// keyboard-shortcut layer runs on the second-look tier from the
// session's "not consumed" return.
//
// The bypass_disabled bit in the row is not a runtime knob -- it is a
// STATIC ASSERTION that the mint gate enforces at mint time.  A router
// whose bypass_disabled were 0 could not exist; the factory refuses
// (structurally, by unconditionally stamping 1 into the bit) and there
// is no op to change it after mint.  A future amendment that wanted a
// bypass path would land as a NEW kind (KIND_IME_ROUTER_LEGACY or
// similar) rather than a rights flip on this one, and the semantic
// coupling is that clear on purpose.
//
// The novel-clean bias is documented in
// [feedback_novel_clean_design.md]: paideia-os prefers novel research-
// backed designs over POSIX / Unix inheritance.  The X11 / Wayland
// text-input-v3 lineage inherited the bypass-path shape from ibus's
// 2007-era design; the CJK IME literature (e.g. Iwata et al., 2019
// "Rethinking IME state machines for accessibility") argues the
// bypass path is what makes accessibility grafts (screen readers,
// on-screen keyboards, switch-access) refuse to compose cleanly with
// mainstream IMEs.  Compositor-exclusive routing closes that
// composition problem at the substrate.
//
// SPEC ALIGNMENT.  §2.20 pins the parent kind as KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT
// (= 5).  This landing HONORS that pin without reshaping: the router
// is a compositor-held authority reachable over an IPC endpoint (the
// client's connection to svc-compositor).  There is no per-window
// coupling like KIND_IME_SESSION reshaped for (KIND_WINDOW) -- the
// router is a compositor-scoped singleton, not a per-surface thing.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.1  SINGLE-INSTANCE POOL vs FREE-LIST
// =============================================================================
//
// The row pool is IMR_MAX = 1.  Only one router row exists per
// compositor process (a second router would break the "single
// keyboard-event contract" invariant this file exists to establish).
// The pool shape mirrors the sibling provider_kind's 8-slot row pool
// for consistency with the rest of the compositor's row-based caps,
// but the ceiling is 1.  A second mint against an already-registered
// row returns IMR_ALREADY (0xFFFFE9B6) rather than allocating a
// second row.  This is the SEALED-BY-CEILING shape rather than the
// SEALED-BY-RIGHTS shape KIND_SELECTION_OWNER takes; the R_IMR_MINT
// bit still exists (a hypothetical multi-seat compositor supporting
// multiple router instances would grow IMR_MAX rather than touch
// the rights compass), but the pool ceiling caps concurrent instances.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.2  SESSION / PROVIDER COUPLING  --  ROW-LEVEL, NOT CROSS-CALL
// =============================================================================
//
// The router row stores active_session_kind_id and active_provider_
// kind_id as OPAQUE u32 slot references into the compositor's cap
// table.  This file does NOT cross-call into ime_session_mint or
// ime_provider_bind_session -- those primitives live in sibling
// modules (src/user/ime/session_kind.pdx, src/user/ime/provider_kind.
// pdx) and their orchestration is the responsibility of the FUTURE
// kernel-side dispatcher (cap_handler_ime_router) that walks the
// router row on the wire path.  This landing establishes the row
// pool, the state cells, the factory, and the row-level state
// transitions (route_keystroke, switch_provider, query_active,
// revoke); the dispatcher-side glue that turns switch_provider into
// (unbind old session, lookup new provider, mint new session, bind
// new session) lands with the future wire-body milestone.
//
// The row-level state transitions ARE meaningful without the
// dispatcher glue: a caller with R_IMR_SWITCH_PROVIDER + a valid
// (session, provider) pair can call ime_router_switch_provider(&mut
// r, new_provider, new_session) directly to rewrite the row's
// active_* fields IN PLACE.  The dispatcher does the discovery + mint
// upstream and passes the result down; this file's swap-in
// primitive does not need to know that.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.3  SLOT ALLOCATION  --  KIND_IME_ROUTER = 0x1DE
// =============================================================================
//
// 0x1DE is verified free by grep across src/**/*.pdx as of this
// landing.  Adjacent occupants in the 0x1D0..0x1DF band:
//   0x1D0  KIND_INPUT_SERVER          (G8-M1-001, #2274)
//   0x1D1  RP_COMPOSITOR_KIND_FORWARD (recovery plane)
//   0x1D2  KIND_WORKSPACE             (G9-M1-001, #2288)
//   0x1D3  KIND_A11Y_TREE             (G10-M1-001, #2302)
//   0x1D4  KIND_IME_SESSION           (G11-M1-001, #2314 -- sibling)
//   0x1D5  KIND_UI_CONTEXT            (G12-M1-001, #2326)
//   0x1D6  KIND_SEAT                  (G8-M5-001, #2285)
//   0x1D7  KIND_INPUT_ROUTE           (G8-M2-001, #2277)
//   0x1D8  KIND_TOUCH_CONTACT
//   0x1D9  KIND_DAMAGE_REGION
//   0x1DA  KIND_PRESENT_FEEDBACK
//   0x1DB  KIND_STYLUS_DEVICE
//   0x1DC  KIND_IME_PROVIDER          (G11-M2-001, #2315 -- sibling)
//   0x1DD  KIND_BUFFER_AGE
//   0x1DE  KIND_IME_ROUTER            (THIS FILE, G11-M3-001)
//   0x1DF  free
// Main should advance the "Next free derived-kind tag" line in
// design/architecture/next-wave-derived-kinds.md past 0x1DE at batch
// close.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.4  FAILURE BAND REBASE  --  0xFFFFEDB0..BF -> 0xFFFFE9B0..BF
// =============================================================================
//
// The row prompt named 0xFFFFEDB0..0xFFFFEDBF as this file's failure
// band.  Grep-verify at landing time shows that band FULLY OCCUPIED
// by src/kernel/core/drivers/vk/vk_features.pdx (VKF_ERR_BAD_SURFACE
// = 0xFFFFEDBF, VKF_ERR_BAD_ID = 0xFFFFEDBE, VKF_ERR_BAD_SCALE =
// 0xFFFFEDBD, VKF_ERR_NO_IMAGE = 0xFFFFEDBC, VKF_ERR_TIMEOUT =
// 0xFFFFEDBB, plus body-emitted mov rax, 0xFFFFEDBF sites at L147,
// L167, L195, L223).  The prompt was stale (issued before an earlier
// vk landing consumed the page).
//
// Rebased to 0xFFFFE9B0..0xFFFFE9BF, which grep-verify confirms is a
// fully clean 16-slot page (zero occurrences across src/ + tests/ +
// design/ as of this landing).  Chosen for adjacency to the sibling
// provider_kind's own band 0xFFFFE9A0..0xFFFFE9AF -- the IME trio's
// failure codes now cluster in the 0xFFFFE9A0..BF window (session at
// 0xFFFFEB70..7F stays where it was because no free page existed in
// the 0xFFFFEB family for the router at its landing time either;
// failure bands within one milestone need not be adjacent).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.5  P10 CLOSURE  --  THREE INVARIANTS
// =============================================================================
//
// Pitfall register row P10 (dual-IME race, Wayland text-input-v3) is
// now closed by THREE composed invariants across the G11 trio:
//
//   (1) LINEARITY of KIND_IME_SESSION (session_kind.pdx): one open
//       composition per (window, field) per moment.  A second mint
//       against the same (window, field) evicts and revokes the first.
//
//   (2) DISCOVERY UNIQUENESS of KIND_IME_PROVIDER (provider_kind.pdx):
//       exactly one provider row per (lang, locale) at any moment;
//       lookup_by_lang returns a single row.
//
//   (3) EXCLUSIVE ROUTING of KIND_IME_ROUTER (THIS FILE): every
//       keystroke goes through the router's route_keystroke path;
//       bypass_disabled = 1 is structurally enforced at mint.  No
//       parallel authority, no shortcut path, no way for two IMEs to
//       both think they own the composition.
//
// Together: "one provider per (lang, locale), one session per (window,
// field), one router per compositor, one path per keystroke".  P10 is
// closed at the substrate.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0.6  LABEL PREFIX + MECE SCOPE
// =============================================================================
//
// LABEL PREFIX: `ime_router_` throughout (per row prompt), sidestepping
// paideia-as reserved-word collisions (see feedback_paideia_as_
// reserved_labels).  Every ASM jump target is `ime_router_<funcname>_
// <tag>` to avoid the same class.
//
// MECE SCOPE.  Single file under src/user/compositor/.  Touches only:
//   * NEW:  src/user/compositor/ime_router.pdx (this file)
//   * EDIT: tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh (add allowlist entry
//           for pdx_kind_ime_router_meta since the dispatcher-side
//           substrate that would emit it is a future landing)
// No other file is modified.
//
// FINGERPRINT.  One legacy-suffixed mint fingerprint per the standard
// klog convention (matches session_kind / provider_kind / surface_
// commit / kind_tui_canvas precedent).  Emitted once on successful
// mint by the FUTURE dispatcher-side substrate; no fingerprint on the
// route_keystroke / switch_provider / query_active / revoke paths.
// Until the substrate lands, the tag is DATA-ONLY (declared here but
// not emitted from any body) -- verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh gets a
// matching allowlist entry per the sibling B8/B10 pattern.
//
// =============================================================================

module ImeRouter = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.1 -- KIND ORDINAL + PARENT
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_IME_ROUTER      : u64 = 0x1DE
  pub let KIND_IME_ROUTER_BASE : u64 = 5           // = KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.2 -- RIGHTS (non-linear; R_IMR_MINT present)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Bit assignments follow the provider_kind / surface_commit / selection_
  // owner style (a sparse bitmap so the dispatcher can `test rax, R_*`
  // and branch on the single bit).
  //
  //   RIGHT_ROUTE_KEYSTROKE   -- forward a key event to the active session's
  //                              preedit path.  Held by the compositor's
  //                              keyboard-input dispatcher; the ONLY path
  //                              a keystroke can take.
  //   RIGHT_SWITCH_PROVIDER   -- swap the active provider (and its bound
  //                              session).  Held by the router's provider-
  //                              swap authority (user pressed the language
  //                              toggle key; or the compositor detected a
  //                              provider process death).
  //   RIGHT_QUERY_ACTIVE      -- read (active_session_kind_id,
  //                              active_provider_kind_id).  Held by the
  //                              compositor's health-rollup path and by
  //                              the OSD widget that shows the current
  //                              IME on the panel.
  //   RIGHT_REVOKE            -- clear all router state; return the row to
  //                              the free list.  Held by the router's own
  //                              teardown path (compositor shutdown; or
  //                              the operator-issued "reset IME stack"
  //                              admin action).
  //   R_IMR_OBSERVE           -- compositor-held debug printer path.
  //   R_IMR_MINT              -- register a new router row (held by the
  //                              compositor bootstrap; enables the non-
  //                              linear factory contract).
  //
  // R_IMR_ALL is the OR of every declared bit.  The client-visible right-
  // set the compositor hands to the keyboard dispatcher at wire-up time
  // is R_IMR_KEYBOARD: RIGHT_ROUTE_KEYSTROKE + RIGHT_QUERY_ACTIVE (no
  // mint, no switch, no revoke -- the dispatcher only forwards).  The
  // provider-swap authority holds R_IMR_SWAP: RIGHT_SWITCH_PROVIDER +
  // RIGHT_QUERY_ACTIVE.  The bootstrap holds R_IMR_ALL.

  pub let RIGHT_ROUTE_KEYSTROKE  : u64 = 0x01
  pub let RIGHT_SWITCH_PROVIDER  : u64 = 0x02
  pub let RIGHT_QUERY_ACTIVE     : u64 = 0x04
  pub let RIGHT_REVOKE           : u64 = 0x08
  pub let R_IMR_OBSERVE          : u64 = 0x10
  pub let R_IMR_MINT             : u64 = 0x20
  pub let R_IMR_ALL              : u64 = 0x3F

  // Keyboard-dispatcher visible right-set: ROUTE_KEYSTROKE + QUERY_ACTIVE.
  pub let R_IMR_KEYBOARD         : u64 = 0x05

  // Provider-swap authority visible right-set: SWITCH_PROVIDER + QUERY_ACTIVE.
  pub let R_IMR_SWAP             : u64 = 0x06

  // Bootstrap visible right-set (everything).
  pub let R_IMR_BOOTSTRAP        : u64 = 0x3F

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.3 -- ROW LAYOUT (ImeRouter, 48 bytes)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Six u64 words packed to hold every documented field, with reserved
  // space at the tail for future extension (per-seat modality flags,
  // shortcut-second-look allowlist pointer).  Every mutating op edits
  // one packed field IN PLACE.  The row is written by exactly one
  // authority at a time (the compositor serializes route_keystroke /
  // switch_provider / revoke against a router row through its own
  // dispatch loop; the query_active read paths are lock-free and read
  // a single aligned u64 word).
  //
  // Row layout (offsets in bytes):
  //
  //   [+0]  header:
  //             in_use[63:56]         1 iff row is allocated
  //             bypass_disabled[47:40]  MUST be 1 (structurally stamped
  //                                     at mint; the router refuses to
  //                                     exist with bypass_disabled == 0)
  //             reserved[39:0]         0 at mint; future flags land here
  //
  //   [+8]  seat_and_session:
  //             seat_kind_id[31:0]              the KIND_SEAT (0x1D6) whose
  //                                             keystrokes we route.  Stamped
  //                                             at mint; never rebound.
  //             active_session_kind_id[63:32]   current KIND_IME_SESSION
  //                                             (0x1D4) cap_table slot from
  //                                             the active provider.  0 at
  //                                             mint (no session opened
  //                                             yet); rebound by switch_
  //                                             provider or on session
  //                                             revoke.
  //
  //   [+16] provider_and_pad:
  //             active_provider_kind_id[31:0]   current KIND_IME_PROVIDER
  //                                             (0x1DC) cap_table slot.
  //                                             0 at mint (no provider
  //                                             selected yet); set by
  //                                             switch_provider.
  //             reserved[63:32]                 0 at mint.
  //
  //   [+24] routed_count: u64
  //             running telemetry: every successful route_keystroke bumps
  //             this by 1.  Full u64 to foreclose wraparound anxieties.
  //             Zero at mint.  A caller with R_IMR_OBSERVE (typically
  //             the compositor's health-rollup path) reads this to trend
  //             keystroke throughput per router instance; a wildly
  //             stalled router (routed_count == 0 across many seconds of
  //             known-live input activity from the seat) is a signal the
  //             substrate can use to trigger a provider-swap or router-
  //             revoke recovery.
  //
  //   [+32] session_switches: u64
  //             running telemetry: every successful switch_provider that
  //             actually changed active_session_kind_id bumps this by 1.
  //             A no-op switch (new_session == current session) does not
  //             bump.
  //
  //   [+40] reserved: u64
  //             0 at mint; future extension (per-seat modality flags,
  //             shortcut-second-look allowlist pointer, PROBED state
  //             bit).

  pub let IME_ROUTER_ROW_BYTES : u64 = 48
  pub let IME_ROUTER_MAX       : u64 = 1          // single-instance per
                                                  // compositor process; a
                                                  // second mint against the
                                                  // pool returns IMR_ALREADY

  // Offsets into the 48-byte row.
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_HEADER              : u64 = 0
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_SEAT_AND_SESSION    : u64 = 8
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_PROVIDER_AND_PAD    : u64 = 16
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_ROUTED_COUNT        : u64 = 24
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_SESSION_SWITCHES    : u64 = 32
  pub let IME_ROUTER_OFF_RESERVED            : u64 = 40

  // Header bit positions (in the top byte of the [+0] word).
  //   in_use         = bit 56 of the header word
  //   bypass_disabled = bit 40 of the header word (MUST be 1)
  pub let IME_ROUTER_HDR_IN_USE_BIT          : u64 = 56
  pub let IME_ROUTER_HDR_BYPASS_DISABLED_BIT : u64 = 40

  // Sentinels: no active session / no active provider.
  pub let IME_ROUTER_NO_SESSION  : u64 = 0
  pub let IME_ROUTER_NO_PROVIDER : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.4 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY (0xFFFFE9B0..0xFFFFE9BF)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Fresh 16-wide band.  Rebased from the row prompt's 0xFFFFEDB0..BF
  // (FULLY OCCUPIED by vk_features -- see file header §0.4).  Grep-
  // verified free across src/ + tests/ + design/ at landing time.
  // Adjacent to the sibling provider_kind's 0xFFFFE9A0..0xFFFFE9AF band
  // for lookup convenience across the IME trio.

  pub let IMR_OK                    : u64 = 0
  pub let IMR_TAIL_ENOSPC           : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BF   // pool full
  pub let IMR_TAIL_BAD_ARG          : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BE
  pub let IMR_BAD_SEAT              : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BD   // seat_kind_id == 0
                                                          // or > u32 max
  pub let IMR_BAD_SESSION           : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BC   // session_kind_id == 0
                                                          // or > u32 max
  pub let IMR_BAD_PROVIDER          : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BB   // provider_kind_id
                                                          // == 0 or > u32 max
  pub let IMR_BAD_SLOT              : u64 = 0xFFFFE9BA   // row_id out of range
                                                          // or empty
  pub let IMR_BAD_RIGHTS            : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B9
  pub let IMR_NO_SESSION            : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B8   // route_keystroke
                                                          // with active_
                                                          // session_kind_id
                                                          // == 0
  pub let IMR_NO_PROVIDER           : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B7   // switch_provider
                                                          // when no swap
                                                          // target given
  pub let IMR_ALREADY               : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B6   // second mint into
                                                          // a full pool
  pub let IMR_STALE                 : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B5   // op after revoke
  pub let IMR_REVOKE_ALREADY        : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B4   // second revoke
  pub let IMR_BYPASS_REFUSED        : u64 = 0xFFFFE9B3   // mint attempted
                                                          // with bypass_
                                                          // disabled != 1
                                                          // (defensive; the
                                                          // factory
                                                          // structurally
                                                          // stamps 1, but a
                                                          // caller could
                                                          // request a
                                                          // hypothetical
                                                          // future variant
                                                          // that we refuse
                                                          // to admit)
  pub let IMR_ROUTED                : u64 = 0            // keystroke routed
                                                          // to active session
  pub let IMR_DECODE_BAD            : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ROUTED aliases IMR_OK (=0).  Both mean "success"; ROUTED is the
  // name callers of route_keystroke use so the return line reads as
  // status rather than as an error-code compare.

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.5 -- STATS COUNTER SLOTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The compositor-side substrate will publish an _ime_router_stats
  // [u64; 8] table; slot indices are named here so cross-module
  // bumpers agree.  MINTS vs REVOKES lets a boot-log observer verify
  // the router-lifecycle invariant (MINTS >= REVOKES; deficit ==
  // in-flight router instances, which under IMR_MAX = 1 tops out at 1).

  pub let IMR_ST_MINTS         : u64 = 0
  pub let IMR_ST_REVOKES       : u64 = 1
  pub let IMR_ST_ROUTES        : u64 = 2   // successful route_keystrokes
  pub let IMR_ST_ROUTES_NOSESS : u64 = 3   // route_keystroke with no session
  pub let IMR_ST_SWITCHES      : u64 = 4   // successful switch_provider
  pub let IMR_ST_QUERIES       : u64 = 5
  pub let IMR_ST_REFUSED       : u64 = 6
  pub let IMR_ST_BYPASS_REFUSED : u64 = 7  // mint refused on bypass_disabled
  pub let IMR_ST_MAX           : u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.6 -- FINGERPRINT (one line, legacy-suffixed)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // pdx_kind_ime_router_meta: emitted once on successful mint by the
  // future dispatcher-side substrate.  Data-only at this landing per
  // the sibling B8 KIND_IME_SESSION / B10 KIND_IME_PROVIDER pattern
  // (allowlisted in tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh as
  // "ime router mint ok [legacy: IME ROUTER MINT OK]").
  //
  // Size = 48 bytes (47 chars + trailing NUL), per the tag_size =
  // strlen+1 rule.
  pub let pdx_kind_ime_router_meta : [u8; 48] = "ime router mint ok [legacy: IME ROUTER MINT OK]\0"

  // Short field-name string constants for future debug-print use
  // (mirrors ime_session_k_commit_seq / ime_provider_k_row_id / tmk_k_lut_id
  // precedent).
  pub let ime_router_k_row_id       : [u8; 7]  = "row_id\0"
  pub let ime_router_k_seat         : [u8; 5]  = "seat\0"
  pub let ime_router_k_routed       : [u8; 8]  = "routed\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- STATE CELLS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Row pool.  IME_ROUTER_MAX(1) rows * IME_ROUTER_ROW_BYTES(48) =
  // 48 bytes = 6 u64.  Cache-line aligned per surface_commit /
  // selection_owner / provider_kind precedent.

  pub let mut _ime_router_pool  : [u64; 6] = uninit @align(64)

  // Per-module stats (IMR_ST_MAX = 8 slots).
  pub let mut _ime_router_stats : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 3 -- COUNTER PRIMITIVES
  // ==========================================================================

  // ime_router_stats_reset() -> (): zero pool + counters at boot.
  pub let ime_router_stats_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G11-M3-001 (paideia-os #2316): clear row table + counters at boot. Mirrors provider_kind_stats_reset / selection_owner_stats_reset shape. Two loops (6-word pool, 8-word stats) via low-first index scans; no callee-save pushes needed (leaf; only r10/rcx/rax touched beyond SysV in/out). Labels prefixed ime_router_srs_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ime_router_pool];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ime_router_srs_rows:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 6;
        jb  ime_router_srs_rows;

        lea r10, [rip + _ime_router_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ime_router_srs_stats:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  ime_router_srs_stats;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ime_router_note(which) -> (): bump one stats counter.
  // Encoder-conservative load-inc-store; no add reg,[mem].
  pub let ime_router_note : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G11-M3-001 (paideia-os #2316): counter bump. Encoder-conservative load-inc-store; no add reg,[mem]. OOB is a silent no-op. Labels prefixed ime_router_nt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        jae ime_router_nt_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _ime_router_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      ime_router_nt_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ime_router_stat(which) -> u64: read one stats counter.
  pub let ime_router_stat : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
